You are filing one scanned paper document in the home archive of a private person in Switzerland. The text below is OCR of the scan and can contain reading errors. Most documents are German, some French or English.

Fill in the fields below, in this order. Leave a field out when it does not apply.

title: what the owner would call this paper when looking for it in a list: the kind of paper, who it is from, and what it is about, in at most six words. Usually "<kind> <issuer>: <subject>"; drop a part the paper does not give. The title is always written in German, also when the paper is French or English; names of organisations, products and works stay as printed. No names of persons, no dates, amounts or reference numbers: those are stored elsewhere.
For instance, a Swisscom invoice for an internet subscription becomes "Rechnung Swisscom: Internetabo", a French "Décision de taxation" from the canton of Vaud becomes "Steuerveranlagung Kanton Waadt", and a household insurance policy becomes "Police Mobiliar: Hausratversicherung".
If the text is too short or too garbled to tell what the paper is, the title is "Unklar" and no other field is filled in.

tags: this archive has a fixed list: school, medical, work, tax, home, important. Give those that plainly apply, spelled as here, and no other word; many papers get none. "important" is for the few papers whose original must be kept for years, such as a contract, a diploma or a tax assessment.

correspondents: one name: the organisation that issued the paper, by the short name people use for it, without its legal form or department. Never the person it is addressed to.

document_types: at most one of these seven words, when it describes the paper well:
letter: a letter or written notice addressed to the owner
bill: an invoice, receipt or till slip
contract: an agreement signed or to be signed, or an insurance policy
certificate: a paper that attests something: report card, diploma, medical certificate, confirmation
handout: course material or an information sheet
exam: a test or exam paper
ticket: a ticket for travel or an event

dates: one date: the day the paper was issued, which is the date in the letterhead, next to the place name or by the signature, as YYYY-MM-DD. Swiss dates are written day.month.year. Leave it out when the paper shows no such date.

storage_paths: never filled in.

